{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "operational_burden",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "Describes the type/domain of the incident (database, email, VPN), not an aspect of the burden it imposes. It classifies what broke, which is a predictor of burden, not a part of it."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_symptom",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "Captures the nature of the failure (connectivity, access denied, outage). This characterizes the fault, not the operational burden as an aspect; it is a driver of burden rather than a constitutive part."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_resource_scope",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Where the burden falls (server-side, network, client-side) is a genuine dimension of operational burden -- which part of the operation must be engaged. Borderline but part-of the burden's locus."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_blast_radius",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Scope of impact (individual vs system-wide vs location) is a constitutive aspect of how large the operational burden is -- an impact-magnitude facet."
    },
    {
      "name": "self_resolved",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Whether the incident resolved without intervention directly speaks to the effort/handling burden -- a facet of the operational load actually incurred."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrent_caller",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "A property of the caller, not an aspect of the burden. It may correlate with burden but is not a constitutive part of it."
    },
    {
      "name": "off_hours_incident",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Timing (off-hours) is a facet of operational burden because it modulates the cost/effort of handling -- a temporal-load aspect of the burden."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Recurrence is a genuine aspect of ongoing operational burden (repeated work), though it overlaps heavily with recurrent_caller in intent."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": ["affected_resource_scope", "incident_blast_radius", "self_resolved", "off_hours_incident", "recurrence_signal"],
  "non_facet_columns": ["incident_category", "failure_symptom", "recurrent_caller"],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.55,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.5,
  "rationale": "Only a subset of the added columns are true meronymic facets of operational_burden. incident_category and failure_symptom describe what/how the incident failed -- external predictors that classify the fault rather than aspects of the burden itself. recurrent_caller is a caller attribute. Among the facet-like columns, recurrence_signal and recurrent_caller substantially overlap (both encode recurrence), and off_hours_incident, blast_radius, and self_resolved each touch different aspects but overlap loosely on 'handling effort', lowering mutual exclusivity. Exhaustiveness is moderate: the set touches impact scope, locus, timing, resolution effort, and recurrence, but omits central burden dimensions such as time-to-resolve / effort-hours, staffing/escalation cost, SLA/priority pressure, and reopen/backlog load. As a whole the column set leans toward classifying incidents rather than cleanly decomposing the burden concept into MECE constitutive parts."
}

